94
00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:54.430
SDI and HDMI, what's the big difference?

95
00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:57.470
Well, first of all, you should realize

96
00:04:57.470 --> 00:05:01.670
that HDMI has some specific limitations,

97
00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:05.350
namely how far of a signal it can actually transmit

98
00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:09.189
without having to be augmented by some sort of booster.

99
00:05:09.189 --> 00:05:13.589
SDI can run considerably longer cable lengths than HDMI.

100
00:05:13.589 --> 00:05:16.629
And so SDI is the standard that we typically use

101
00:05:16.629 --> 00:05:18.970
within an ICV effects volume.

102
00:05:19.009 --> 00:05:21.649
Because of that, there are a number of different types

103
00:05:21.649 --> 00:05:23.649
of SDI that you should be aware of.

104
00:05:23.649 --> 00:05:28.649
There's HD SDI, 3G SDI, 6G SDI, and 12G SDI.

105
00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:34.970
Each of these have a different bandwidth and signal rate

106
00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:37.850
and are used for specific resolutions

107
00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:39.850
that you might want to output to.

108
00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:43.430
So as you can see on this particular slide,

109
00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:46.810
HD SDI is a 720p signal,

110
00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:50.970
and it's only transmitting at 1.485 gigabits per second.

111
00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:55.970
Whereas a 12G SDI signal is, it's rated for 2160p

112
00:05:58.459 --> 00:06:02.019
and has a 12 gigabit per second bandwidth.

113
00:06:02.019 --> 00:06:05.660
So considerably more information and more resolution

114
00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:10.660
is being sent on these higher class SDI signals.

115
00:06:11.379 --> 00:06:14.139
Now, there's something that you should realize

116
00:06:14.139 --> 00:06:19.480
when hooking up your connections to your camera.

117
00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:23.319
SDI has some very specific warnings

118
00:06:23.319 --> 00:06:26.680
about voltage and potential damage

119
00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:29.319
that can occur to the SDI circuitry

120
00:06:29.319 --> 00:06:31.120
if you hook up your camera

121
00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:34.339
and your SDI devices incorrectly.

122
00:06:34.339 --> 00:06:36.720
You should realize that,

123
00:06:37.769 --> 00:06:41.430
just gonna take a look here in this particular slide,

124
00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:43.670
Bill Bennett from the American Society

125
00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:45.670
of Cinematographers writes this,

126
00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:50.699
HDI, SDI vulnerability across all devices,

127
00:06:51.139 --> 00:06:54.259
cameras and monitors and transmitters

128
00:06:54.259 --> 00:06:58.699
in order to prevent burnout of your SDI circuitry.

129
00:06:58.699 --> 00:07:02.360
When building the camera and connecting all power cables

130
00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:04.660
to all accessories first,

131
00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:08.300
then connect the BNC cables and do the reverse

132
00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:11.379
on teardown, BNC disconnect first

133
00:07:11.379 --> 00:07:13.339
and then unplug the power.

134
00:07:13.699 --> 00:07:15.860
We wanna make sure that when you're setting things up

135
00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:18.839
that you have a grounded electrical source

136
00:07:18.839 --> 00:07:21.660
and power hooked up to the camera first.

137
00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:23.519
If you don't do this

138
00:07:23.519 --> 00:07:27.899
and you're trying to connect the SDI BNC cable

139
00:07:27.899 --> 00:07:29.459
or power out of order,

140
00:07:29.459 --> 00:07:34.040
you can potentially burn out your SDI circuitry.

141
00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:36.860
It's something that I've seen happen personally.

142
00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:38.699
It's not something that you wanna explain

143
00:07:38.699 --> 00:07:42.259
to your director or cinematographer on stage.

144
00:07:42.259 --> 00:07:44.899
So be careful when hooking up your equipment.

145
00:07:44.899 --> 00:07:47.500
All right, LED hardware and processing,

146
00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:51.459
we wanna talk about a little bit more about timecode.

147
00:07:51.459 --> 00:07:53.579
First, what is timecode?

148
00:07:53.579 --> 00:07:56.100
It's basically, it's a system and a signal

149
00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:59.779
that measures time by dividing it up into smaller frames.

150
00:07:59.779 --> 00:08:02.740
So timecode will synchronize the first frame

151
00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:06.819
across all devices as common metadata during recording.

152
00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:08.819
There's basically two types of timecode.

153
00:08:08.819 --> 00:08:12.100
There's LTC, which is linear timecode.

154
00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:15.139
This is basically recorded on the audio track

155
00:08:15.139 --> 00:08:20.459
and it's a little bit easier to implement and use.

156
00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:26.139
But then there's also VTC or vertical interval timecode.

157
00:08:26.139 --> 00:08:28.480
This is where the timecode is actually embedded

158
00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:31.860
into the video signal that's being recorded.

159
00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:34.720
And that has some certain advantages.

160
00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:37.299
There's advantages and disadvantages to both.

161
00:08:37.299 --> 00:08:41.340
Linear timecode doesn't allow you to basically scrub

162
00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:45.860
through the recorded signal where vertical timecode can

163
00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:49.179
and the linear timecode is recorded,

164
00:08:49.179 --> 00:08:50.779
like I said, onto the audio track.

165
00:08:50.779 --> 00:08:53.100
So you have to consider devoting

166
00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:56.750
one of those audio tracks to LTC.

167
00:08:56.750 --> 00:08:59.509
If you look at the devices that we have hooked up

168
00:08:59.509 --> 00:09:02.669
in your facility, you're gonna have a house generator

169
00:09:02.669 --> 00:09:06.649
for both your timecode and for your genlock.

170
00:09:06.649 --> 00:09:08.870
There are basically two different flavors here

171
00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:11.090
of devices that you can utilize.

172
00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:13.169
Again, if you have a dedicated facility,

173
00:09:13.169 --> 00:09:15.889
I recommend the house generator.

174
00:09:15.889 --> 00:09:18.929
And I'm gonna show you what we have over here

175
00:09:18.929 --> 00:09:20.629
in the latte stage.

176
00:09:20.629 --> 00:09:24.169
So if you take a look over on the latte stage here,

177
00:09:24.169 --> 00:09:28.929
this is our house timecode and genlock generator.

178
00:09:28.929 --> 00:09:35.110
It is basically rigged into a number

179
00:09:35.590 --> 00:09:37.230
of the different potential features

180
00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:39.269
and functions of our facility.

181
00:09:39.269 --> 00:09:40.750
And you can see that there are a number

182
00:09:40.789 --> 00:09:43.789
of different connections, B and C connections on the back.

183
00:09:44.629 --> 00:09:46.590
Each of these different wired connections

184
00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:48.289
are going to different devices,

185
00:09:48.289 --> 00:09:50.610
like in this particular case,

186
00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:55.610
this is the OptiTrack MoCap timecode connection.

187
00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:59.370
And these wired connections also will go over to,

188
00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:01.509
as mentioned, the Tessera.

189
00:10:03.029 --> 00:10:05.700
We're a little out of focus here.

190
00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:09.200
Sorry about that.

191
00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:10.039
There we go.

192
00:10:11.039 --> 00:10:14.779
The Brompton processors, the Tessera SX40,

193
00:10:14.779 --> 00:10:16.419
which we have here.

194
00:10:16.419 --> 00:10:19.320
And as also mentioned, wired connections

195
00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:23.120
will also go over to your camera

196
00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:27.710
and to your various control machines.

197
00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:33.190
Now, if you can't afford a house generator like that,

198
00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:36.149
or you're running a more independent solution,

199
00:10:36.149 --> 00:10:40.850
you can use more smaller modular timecode generators.

200
00:10:40.850 --> 00:10:43.789
The Tentacle Sync device is a good option here.

201
00:10:43.789 --> 00:10:47.870
This allows you to hook up to various other cameras

202
00:10:47.870 --> 00:10:51.470
or recorders or any other types of devices

203
00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:55.230
that may not have a dedicated BNC connection.

204
00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:57.429
What's nice about these is that one

205
00:10:57.429 --> 00:11:00.509
of the Tentacle devices can act as a master

206
00:11:00.509 --> 00:11:04.110
and can synchronize to all the other devices

207
00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:05.830
through the use of an iOS device,

208
00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:07.190
like an iPhone.

209
00:11:07.190 --> 00:11:12.190
And this is a great way to potentially offer timecode

210
00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:16.789
and have it wirelessly synchronize all of these devices

211
00:11:16.789 --> 00:11:21.500
without having to have a dedicated wired house generator.

212
00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:24.860
All right, we talked about the process of jamming timecode.

213
00:11:24.860 --> 00:11:27.100
I just want to give a dedicated slide for this,

214
00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:28.500
just so that you can see it.

215
00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:33.419
So some devices only have a timecode connection port

216
00:11:33.419 --> 00:11:37.259
and may lack the necessary BNCs that you'll need.

217
00:11:37.259 --> 00:11:42.100
So every camera, at least every high-end camera,

218
00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:44.179
has its own internal clock.

219
00:11:44.179 --> 00:11:48.679
So that clock can be synchronized with your house timecode

220
00:11:48.679 --> 00:11:50.899
by doing the process of jamming timecode.

221
00:11:50.899 --> 00:11:55.019
That's basically where you hook up the BNC connection

222
00:11:55.019 --> 00:11:59.120
to the timecode port on the back of your camera

223
00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:03.639
and allow the signal to synchronize with the house clock.

224
00:12:03.639 --> 00:12:08.639
Then the camera clock and your timecode clock

225
00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:11.620
will be at the same time.

226
00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:13.840
So it's at that point,

227
00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:18.080
you can disconnect the timecode signal from the camera

228
00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:21.159
and you can then hook up genlock.

229
00:12:21.159 --> 00:12:24.419
If you're, like I said, on the Blackmagic Ursa,

230
00:12:24.419 --> 00:12:27.799
it has both a reference in and a timecode in,

231
00:12:27.799 --> 00:12:29.679
but it's on a single port.

232
00:12:29.679 --> 00:12:31.840
You can just swap and exchange these

233
00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:34.059
as we demonstrated earlier.

234
00:12:34.059 --> 00:12:35.919
The thing to keep in mind with this is,

235
00:12:35.919 --> 00:12:38.919
is that some of the internal clocks on the cameras

236
00:12:38.919 --> 00:12:40.820
can drift over time.

237
00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:43.759
And if that happens, you should make it a regular practice

238
00:12:43.759 --> 00:12:47.299
of jamming timecode multiple times throughout the day,

239
00:12:47.299 --> 00:12:50.200
probably right when you get in to your facility

240
00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:52.399
in the morning, probably once at lunch

241
00:12:52.399 --> 00:12:54.519
and maybe once in the afternoon.

242
00:12:54.519 --> 00:12:58.720
That way you'll ensure that the clock within the camera

243
00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:02.480
stays in sync with your house timecode.

244
00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:03.919
All right, we're gonna continue on

245
00:13:03.919 --> 00:13:07.259
and start talking about the process of jamming timecode.

246
00:13:07.259 --> 00:13:09.559
We demonstrated this a little bit earlier,

247
00:13:09.559 --> 00:13:11.039
but I just wanna show the slide

248
00:13:11.039 --> 00:13:14.039
to make sure that you fully understand the process.

249
00:13:14.039 --> 00:13:18.720
So if your camera has only a single BNC connector

250
00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:22.360
that handles reference in and timecode,

251
00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:26.240
you're gonna have to do the process of jamming timecode.

252
00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:29.080
So every camera, every professional camera

253
00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:31.759
will typically have an internal clock.

254
00:13:31.759 --> 00:13:34.840
And that internal clock is very accurate

255
00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:38.600
and capable of receiving a timecode signal.

256
00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:41.639
And the process of jamming timecode

257
00:13:41.639 --> 00:13:44.360
is essentially hooking up the BNC connector

258
00:13:44.360 --> 00:13:46.320
to the timecode port in,

259
00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:48.440
allowing the internal clock of the camera

260
00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:51.019
to synchronize with the house timecode.

261
00:13:51.019 --> 00:13:54.379
And then you unplug the timecode cable

262
00:13:54.379 --> 00:13:56.860
and replace it with the genlock cable.

263
00:13:56.860 --> 00:13:59.179
At that particular point in time,

264
00:13:59.179 --> 00:14:02.019
it'll switch over to genlocking the camera

265
00:14:02.019 --> 00:14:05.179
and your timecode will still be in sync.

266
00:14:05.179 --> 00:14:10.179
Now, to be fair, some cameras will drift over time.

267
00:14:11.659 --> 00:14:13.940
Some cameras don't drift very much.

268
00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:15.500
Some cameras drift a lot.

269
00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:18.139
It just depends on the maker and manufacturer.

270
00:14:18.139 --> 00:14:20.980
So recommended practice would be

271
00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:23.360
when you come into your facility in the morning,

272
00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:26.740
you jam timecode, get the camera clock in sync

273
00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:28.460
with your house timecode,

274
00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:32.519
and then jam timecode again, say at lunch,

275
00:14:32.519 --> 00:14:35.340
and then maybe once later in the afternoon.

276
00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:38.500
This will help accommodate for any potential drift

277
00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:39.779
in the camera clock

278
00:14:39.779 --> 00:14:43.019
and keep you in sync with your house timecode.

279
00:14:43.019 --> 00:14:44.759
All right, we're gonna keep going

280
00:14:44.759 --> 00:14:48.210
and we're gonna start talking about genlock.

281
00:14:48.769 --> 00:14:51.649
Genlock is the other signal that you should be aware of

282
00:14:51.649 --> 00:14:54.730
that's critical for your operations and ICD effects.

283
00:14:54.730 --> 00:14:56.330
So what is genlock?

284
00:14:56.330 --> 00:15:00.809
It too is a common technique where the video output

285
00:15:00.809 --> 00:15:04.289
of one source is used as a timing signal

286
00:15:04.289 --> 00:15:07.970
to synchronize the drawing of all the picture sources

287
00:15:07.970 --> 00:15:11.309
within your facility, pixel by pixel, line by line.

288
00:15:11.309 --> 00:15:13.629
We want everything to be drawn together.

289
00:15:13.629 --> 00:15:15.830
This helps prevent delay and drift

290
00:15:15.830 --> 00:15:17.710
between various sources.

291
00:15:18.149 --> 00:15:20.389
So genlock was a technique that was developed

292
00:15:20.389 --> 00:15:22.610
early in the broadcast situation

293
00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:26.289
where various cameras needed to be in sync with one another

294
00:15:26.289 --> 00:15:29.529
when switching with a camera switcher.

295
00:15:29.529 --> 00:15:34.129
There are basically two different types of genlock signals.

296
00:15:34.129 --> 00:15:36.230
Number one, there is the black burst signal.

297
00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:38.350
This is the older type of signal.

298
00:15:38.350 --> 00:15:40.429
And you can see that there's the waveform vector

299
00:15:40.429 --> 00:15:41.669
up at the top.

300
00:15:41.669 --> 00:15:46.029
Black burst signal is only capable of handling genlock

301
00:15:46.029 --> 00:15:48.610
in two frame rates, 30 frames per second

302
00:15:48.610 --> 00:15:49.970
or 60 frames per second.

303
00:15:49.970 --> 00:15:51.950
So keep that in mind.

304
00:15:51.950 --> 00:15:54.750
The second type of signal is the tri-level sync

305
00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:55.909
genlock signal.

306
00:15:55.909 --> 00:16:00.649
This is for newer devices and can support more alterations

307
00:16:00.649 --> 00:16:01.490
to frame rate.

308
00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:06.490
It'll support 24p along with 2997, 30, 60, et cetera.

309
00:16:06.789 --> 00:16:10.350
Most modern cameras can receive a genlock signal

310
00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:13.909
from either black burst or from tri-level sync,

311
00:16:13.909 --> 00:16:17.830
but it would be recommended that you use

312
00:16:17.830 --> 00:16:22.210
a more modern device, especially for faster frame rates,

313
00:16:22.210 --> 00:16:25.610
variable frame rates and higher resolutions.

314
00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:29.870
Okay, now, when we're talking about genlock,

315
00:16:29.870 --> 00:16:35.320
this is again something that you should be aware of.

316
00:16:35.399 --> 00:16:38.940
Genlock is particularly required within an LED volume

317
00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:43.679
in order to keep everything drawing in sync up on the wall.

318
00:16:43.679 --> 00:16:46.320
And you can see by this particular slide here,

319
00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:49.320
what happens if you are not in genlock.

320
00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:54.399
So your entire LED wall is typically being rendered

321
00:16:54.399 --> 00:16:55.980
by a number of different nodes,

322
00:16:55.980 --> 00:16:58.460
especially if you have a very large wall.

323
00:16:58.460 --> 00:17:00.460
You could have potentially up to five

324
00:17:00.460 --> 00:17:03.139
or six different nodes rendering different sections

325
00:17:03.139 --> 00:17:04.259
of the wall.

326
00:17:04.259 --> 00:17:08.240
If those nodes aren't within end display,

327
00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:11.619
if those nodes aren't synchronized via genlock,

328
00:17:11.619 --> 00:17:13.240
you'll start to see what's happening here.

329
00:17:13.240 --> 00:17:15.720
The ball in the bouncing demonstration there

330
00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:19.440
will start to split and this is not what you want.

331
00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:24.440
In order to prevent this, we deploy the Quadro II card

332
00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:28.380
and you can see that's an image of what it looks like.

333
00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:31.920
It sits and resides inside of your rendering machines

334
00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:34.819
and it has connectors across the top

335
00:17:34.819 --> 00:17:39.720
that hook into ports on your A6000 or A5000 cards

336
00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:41.200
within your computer.

337
00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:46.640
This will then inform the graphics card of when to draw.

338
00:17:47.319 --> 00:17:50.720
And you can also see on the backside of the card

339
00:17:50.720 --> 00:17:53.259
that there are some ethernet connections.

340
00:17:53.259 --> 00:17:54.859
Those ethernet connections,

341
00:17:54.859 --> 00:17:56.960
along with the coax connection there,

342
00:17:58.400 --> 00:18:03.400
it will transmit your genlock signal

343
00:18:03.440 --> 00:18:08.079
that you've hooked up into the card via a BNC connector

344
00:18:08.079 --> 00:18:11.400
and then it will route that genlock signal

345
00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:16.319
via an ethernet port connection to all remaining cards

346
00:18:16.319 --> 00:18:18.000
within your facility.

347
00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:20.960
So you need to have this set up to make sure

348
00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:22.859
that all of your render devices

349
00:18:22.859 --> 00:18:25.589
are in sync with one another.

350
00:18:25.589 --> 00:18:28.789
Latency, this is an issue that occurs

351
00:18:28.789 --> 00:18:32.509
and a lot of people have a lot of concern over it.

352
00:18:32.509 --> 00:18:35.990
It's how fast your ICV effects wall

353
00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:38.390
will actually display information

354
00:18:38.990 --> 00:18:42.269
and how well it responds to camera movement,

355
00:18:42.269 --> 00:18:45.390
tracking and processing of all of your mocap data

356
00:18:45.390 --> 00:18:48.230
and anything else that's being fed

357
00:18:48.230 --> 00:18:50.109
into the system via Unreal.

358
00:18:50.109 --> 00:18:51.890
So there are a number of different things

359
00:18:51.890 --> 00:18:55.930
that can potentially influence latency within the system.

360
00:18:55.930 --> 00:19:00.930
Now at the NAND and L-A-R-N-D stage,

361
00:19:01.190 --> 00:19:04.710
we have a latency of approximately seven frames,

362
00:19:04.710 --> 00:19:07.150
which is pretty good.

363
00:19:07.150 --> 00:19:09.730
Now here at the Latte Lab,

364
00:19:09.730 --> 00:19:13.910
our latency is actually more like around probably 11 frames,

365
00:19:13.910 --> 00:19:16.869
which is starting to become rather undesirable,

366
00:19:16.869 --> 00:19:19.430
but we only have one render node

367
00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:23.670
rendering everything for our particular wall.

368
00:19:23.670 --> 00:19:25.710
So let's take a look at these items

369
00:19:25.710 --> 00:19:27.630
and what contributes to latency.

370
00:19:27.630 --> 00:19:29.930
One, camera tracking delay.

371
00:19:29.930 --> 00:19:33.579
So we talk about having an OptiTrack system

372
00:19:33.579 --> 00:19:35.940
in this particular facility.

373
00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:38.940
All the data from those cameras you're tracking,

374
00:19:38.940 --> 00:19:42.500
whether you're tracking the tracking mind on the camera,

375
00:19:42.500 --> 00:19:45.299
all that data has to be processed in Motive

376
00:19:45.299 --> 00:19:47.500
and then Motive has to send

377
00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:50.019
a camera tracking solution into Unreal.

378
00:19:50.019 --> 00:19:52.099
So there's a certain amount of time

379
00:19:52.099 --> 00:19:55.259
and processing that that takes to occur.

380
00:19:55.259 --> 00:19:57.819
Second, when it gets into Unreal,

381
00:19:57.819 --> 00:20:00.380
Unreal has to take it, understand it

382
00:20:00.380 --> 00:20:04.380
and apply it to the virtual camera inside of Unreal.

383
00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:07.579
Then there's the issue of actually rendering out

384
00:20:07.579 --> 00:20:09.460
what's in your scene.

385
00:20:09.460 --> 00:20:13.619
So your GPU performance will come into a factor there,

386
00:20:13.619 --> 00:20:15.680
whether you're using A6000 cards

387
00:20:15.680 --> 00:20:20.460
or potentially something slower like A4000 or A5000 cards

388
00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:25.460
or earlier cards like the Quadro 6000 RTX,

389
00:20:25.559 --> 00:20:27.380
there's a number of different cards

390
00:20:27.380 --> 00:20:30.059
that can be used in ICV effects volume.

391
00:20:30.059 --> 00:20:34.140
So you wanna have the fastest GPU possible.

392
00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:37.329
Second, video signal routing and conversion.

393
00:20:37.329 --> 00:20:38.990
All of the different converters

394
00:20:38.990 --> 00:20:40.670
that you may have in your facility,

395
00:20:40.670 --> 00:20:42.529
whether you're converting the video signal

396
00:20:42.529 --> 00:20:43.730
out of your camera,

397
00:20:43.730 --> 00:20:45.490
or you're converting the video signal

398
00:20:45.490 --> 00:20:47.250
out of your graphics cards,

399
00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:48.910
all of these particular devices,

400
00:20:48.910 --> 00:20:51.710
if you're trying to convert their signal,

401
00:20:51.710 --> 00:20:54.289
may add a little bit of delay

402
00:20:54.289 --> 00:20:56.049
in that conversion process.

403
00:20:56.049 --> 00:20:57.809
So be aware of that.

404
00:20:57.809 --> 00:21:01.170
Next, the LED processing and scaling.

405
00:21:01.170 --> 00:21:02.890
Everything that's coming out of Unreal

406
00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:04.069
and out of your render nodes

407
00:21:04.750 --> 00:21:05.869
are gonna be going to these Brompton

408
00:21:05.869 --> 00:21:07.950
or megapixel processors.

409
00:21:07.950 --> 00:21:10.789
These LED processors have to do a certain amount

410
00:21:10.789 --> 00:21:13.190
of processing of the information

411
00:21:13.190 --> 00:21:15.710
and scaling the visual signal.

412
00:21:15.710 --> 00:21:17.910
And that takes a little bit of time.

413
00:21:17.910 --> 00:21:20.009
Then it has to convert everything over

414
00:21:20.009 --> 00:21:22.390
to 10 gigabit ethernet

415
00:21:22.390 --> 00:21:25.589
and transmit it over to the distribution units,

416
00:21:25.589 --> 00:21:28.650
which are feeding the actual panels themselves.

417
00:21:28.650 --> 00:21:31.829
So that transmission time factors into latency.

418
00:21:31.829 --> 00:21:35.289
And then finally, when that particular signal

419
00:21:35.289 --> 00:21:36.789
coming from the distribution nodes

420
00:21:36.789 --> 00:21:38.670
are hitting the individual panels,

421
00:21:38.670 --> 00:21:41.309
the panels have to draw the image itself.

422
00:21:41.309 --> 00:21:44.069
So all of this adds up.

423
00:21:44.069 --> 00:21:47.769
Now, there is a way to potentially turn on

424
00:21:47.769 --> 00:21:51.349
a low latency mode for some of these processors,

425
00:21:51.349 --> 00:21:53.190
like the Brompton has the ability

426
00:21:53.190 --> 00:21:55.470
to turn on a low latency mode

427
00:21:55.470 --> 00:21:59.670
in which it will sacrifice a certain amount of accuracy

428
00:21:59.670 --> 00:22:01.509
in the shadow regions

429
00:22:01.509 --> 00:22:05.630
in order to potentially draw the image faster.

430
00:22:05.630 --> 00:22:09.819
Now, the low latency mode may save you

431
00:22:09.819 --> 00:22:12.039
about one frame per second.

432
00:22:12.039 --> 00:22:14.920
So if you are a little bit on the sluggish side,

433
00:22:14.920 --> 00:22:15.900
you could turn that on

434
00:22:15.900 --> 00:22:18.940
and it might give you a little bit better performance.

435
00:22:18.940 --> 00:22:21.940
Finally, you have the issue of the camera,

436
00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:23.680
its particular shutter,

437
00:22:23.680 --> 00:22:25.700
how fast you're moving the camera

438
00:22:25.700 --> 00:22:28.680
and the frame rate in which you're shooting at.

439
00:22:28.680 --> 00:22:31.200
If you're shooting at 24 frames per second,

440
00:22:31.200 --> 00:22:34.119
seven frames of delay is considerable.

441
00:22:34.119 --> 00:22:37.299
You will feel it just a little bit.

442
00:22:37.299 --> 00:22:39.440
But if you're shooting at higher frame rates,

443
00:22:39.440 --> 00:22:42.880
that seven frames of delay won't be as noticeable.

444
00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:46.220
So tracking system and options.

445
00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:48.640
Just so that you are all familiar,

446
00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:49.559
we've been talking about

447
00:22:49.559 --> 00:22:51.319
all these different tracking solutions

448
00:22:51.319 --> 00:22:53.640
inside of the ICD effects volume.

449
00:22:53.640 --> 00:22:57.039
There's the OptiTrack system using Motive software.

450
00:22:57.039 --> 00:22:59.019
That's what we have here at the Latte stage

451
00:22:59.019 --> 00:23:01.380
and at the LA R&D stage.

452
00:23:01.380 --> 00:23:03.680
But you can use certainly a number

453
00:23:03.680 --> 00:23:05.160
of other different solutions

454
00:23:05.160 --> 00:23:08.799
like the Shogun solution, the Vicon.

455
00:23:08.799 --> 00:23:12.220
This is another alternative that works very well.

456
00:23:12.220 --> 00:23:14.640
You can also, for more independent productions,

457
00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:17.240
you can use the Vive system.

458
00:23:17.240 --> 00:23:20.640
They have their Mars solution that works great.

459
00:23:20.640 --> 00:23:24.079
You can also use just your Vive base stations

460
00:23:24.079 --> 00:23:27.000
and tracking pucks as another option as well.

461
00:23:27.920 --> 00:23:30.759
All three of these will suffice,

462
00:23:30.759 --> 00:23:32.640
but you're probably gonna wanna be using

463
00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:35.119
either the OptiTrack or the Vicon solution

464
00:23:35.119 --> 00:23:36.960
for professional uses.
